# Writing for a connected home

Use this guide when writing a civica dashboard, custom control, help page, notification template or documentation example. Start with the task a person needs to complete and the evidence the system actually has. A clear sentence cannot make an unsupported action reliable.

This is an original household writing standard informed by official public-service guidance, Plain English and SCULPT. It is not an official government, NHS or Home Office service and does not confer their approval.

<h2 id="wg-001">WG-001 — Start with a real household need</h2>

**SHOULD.** State who needs the content, what they need to do or understand, and the context in which they will use it.

A person checking the heating at night needs the current state and a relevant action before an explanation of the integration. A person troubleshooting an installation may need an exact diagnostic name. Neither audience benefits from the same unexplained technical summary.

Put the most useful information first. Give a page a specific title and a short summary that adds context rather than repeating it. Treat publisher character-count suggestions as editing prompts, not reasons to truncate a room name or omit a condition.

<%= example('wg-task-first') %>

<h2 id="wg-002">WG-002 — Apply the guide to content we own</h2>

**MUST.** Change civica-authored labels and explanatory content without silently rewriting native Home Assistant translations, user names or integration values.

A native feature label is part of the person’s navigation map. A household name may be Welsh, abbreviated, humorous or meaningful only to that household. Keep both intact. Explain an unfamiliar native term beside it when necessary. Correcting an authored hint is different from renaming an entity or changing a translated native control.

The guide covers documentation and proposed patterns as well as implemented components. A reference example must not imply that the repository already installs that workflow.

<%= example('wg-scope') %>

<h2 id="wg-003">WG-003 — Use requirement levels deliberately</h2>

**MUST.** Interpret MUST as a required civica content contract, SHOULD as a tested default with a documented reason for an exception, and MAY as an allowed contextual choice.

A MUST about truthful state wording applies whenever that state is shown. A SHOULD about active voice permits “Schedule not saved” where the result matters more than the actor. A MAY about additional explanatory text does not permit hiding essential conditions.

These levels describe this project’s standard. They are not claims about legislation, certification or every native Home Assistant screen. A proposal that conflicts with a MUST needs a revised contract and review, not an unrecorded exception.

<h2 id="wg-004">WG-004 — Keep source-system claims in their proper scope</h2>

**MUST.** Adapt transferable writing principles without importing clinical assurance, government identity, legal rules or unsupported services.

The NHS review standard is useful for ownership and version control. Its clinical approval process is not a household safety assessment. Home Office wording about immigration status is not a household permission model. Plain English and SCULPT inform this original guide; their logos, posters, word lists and endorsement claims are not part of it.

The earlier [Content design introduction](../styles/content.html) remains available as a quick reference. Its URL has not changed.

